\section{Pack and Unpack a Backward Branch}

For the branch in Listing~2, whose opcode is \texttt{0b110'0011} and whose \texttt{funct3} is zero, take sources 5 and 6 and a target 12 bytes before the branch. This is a hand calculation, not VADL code:

\begin{verbatim}
immS = -12   # even and within signed displacement range
imm  = 0xffa # -12 / 2 = -6, represented as 12 bits

word[31]     = 1    # imm[11]
word[7]      = 1    # imm[10], not the low displacement bit
word[30..25] = 0x3f # imm[9..4]
word[11..8]  = 0xa  # imm[3..0]
insert rs1=5, rs2=6, funct3=0, opcode=0x63
result = 0xfe628ae3

decode: collect 0xffa, sign-extend -6, append zero -> -12
\end{verbatim}

Moving a single slice can preserve a round trip when both directions use the same incorrect layout. Keep the expected word as an independent golden test. Include the two range endpoints, zero, an odd displacement, and the out-of-range even values \(-4098\) and \(4096\) to exercise domain checks as well as scattering. Because the raw immediate is twelve bits wide, this particular field is also small enough to test all \(4096\) raw values. Every value should decode to an allowed displacement and re-encode to the same field bits; that property complements the golden test rather than replacing it.

\section{Check More Than Bit Coverage}

Check layout locally for overlap, then check encoding families for ambiguous matches. Equal opcode patterns may be disambiguated by a function field or a field constraint; checking just the mnemonic tells nothing about their binary intersection. Listing~2 deliberately puts an \texttt{[rs1 != rs2]} restriction on its example's BEQ encoding; this is not a requirement of RISC-V. A counterexample with equal indices should therefore be rejected by that example but not treated as invalid by an unmodified RISC-V assembler or decoder. Keep the teaching example and a conformance specification separate.

One review technique is to compare the bits that both candidates fix. If their required values differ at any shared fixed position, no word satisfies both. Otherwise the candidate pair still needs checking: there may be a word that satisfies both fixed patterns but neither operand constraint, or a word that satisfies all conditions and exposes a genuine ambiguity.

An edit to an access function changes not only execution but potentially the immediate range seen by instruction selection, the machine code emitter, and symbolic fixups. A useful generator optimization is to precompute field masks or predecode a scattered value, but it should agree with the format's direct meaning:

\begin{verbatim}
raw = ((word >> 31) & 0x1) << 11
    | ((word >>  7) & 0x1) << 10
    | ((word >> 25) & 0x3f) << 4
    | ((word >>  8) & 0xf)
immS = sign_extend(raw, 12) << 1
\end{verbatim}

This pseudocode is a decoder implementation sketch, not VADL. It gives an optimized mask-and-shift decoder a simple reference for differential tests, while the VADL format remains the source of the mapping and the predicate remains the source of encodability. Section~3.3 describes inference for trivial access functions and explicit predicates and encoders for nontrivial cases. Section~5.1 demonstrates that such formats can describe a range of example architectures, not that every derived format can be inverted or that every pair of instructions is necessarily checked by every prototype generator.
